      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To test our hypothesis that lower intakes of previously identified cardioprotective nutrients would be associated with the coronary epidemic in Central and Eastern Europe."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "We conducted a survey of coronary mortality in 16 countries and diet in 19 countries."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS/SETTING",
          "content": "Countries were placed in four groups with different cultural patterns (Central and Eastern Europe, including Russia; Western Europe and the United States; Mediterranean; and Asian)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Independent predictors of coronary mortality."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STATISTICAL ANALYSES PERFORMED",
          "content": "Means and standard deviations were calculated, and analysis of variance with Bonferroni post hoc tests and backward elimination regression analysis was conducted."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Coronary mortality was highest in Central and Eastern Europe followed by Western Europe and the United States, the Mediterranean countries, and Asia (Japan). The model with folate, fiber, and n-6/n-3 fatty acids explained the majority of variation in coronary mortality (men 86%, women 90%). Most of the variation was explained by folate (men 61%, women 62%). The picture is complicated by the fact that folate, lutein/zeaxanthin, and beta-carotene were highly intercorrelated ( r =0.87 to 0.99)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "A diet low in foods containing folate and carotenoids (beta-carotene and lutein/zeaxanthin) may be a major contributing factor to increased coronary risk observed in the countries of Central and Eastern Europe."
        }
      ]},
